From e07187e4a3824dcbe3ceef3606f6b5d3e74ff596 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 00:31:29 -0800
Subject: [PATCH] feat: delta responses for large reads over the Desktop's SSH
 tunnel [daveey]

Measured 2026-11-13 on the hub: six list/poll reads (sessions sidebar, projects tree, kanban board, sessions list,
cron jobs, runners fleet) carry almost all the bytes the Desktop pulls through its tunnel: 2,052 KB a round, 286 KB
gzipped. Fetched again 20 s later and encoded against the first copy they are 12 KB, for under 8 ms of CPU each.
On a 60-175 KB/s link the polls filled the link.

Nothing changes what a request returns: every request reaches the backend, the handler runs, and the client ends
up with its exact bytes. Only the encoding on the wire changes, and only for a client that asks.

Gateway (hermes_cli/web_delta_response.py, pure ASGI, inside the auth gates): a GET with
`X-Hermes-Delta-Accept: zstd` whose answer is a 200 application/json body of 16 KB-8 MB with a Content-Length and
no Content-Encoding is remembered by hash (64 MB LRU) and sent with `X-Hermes-Body-Hash`; when the
`X-Hermes-Delta-Base` the client named is still remembered and the result is smaller, the body is a zstd frame that
uses that base as its prefix (window sized to base + body, long-distance matching on: a 3.5 MB body with one
changed field is 430 bytes, not the 36 KB of the default window).
- Bodies are remembered per credential (hash of the session-token / Authorization / Cookie headers): a delta's
  length says how much the answer shares with its base, so a base is only usable by the credential it was served
  to. Requests with no credential (the public paths) share a namespace of their own. A credential that changes on
  every request never gets a delta.
- If encoding raises, or the body is not its declared length, the handler's response goes out as it was.
- Other methods (HEAD included), statuses, streams, encoded or small bodies, and a Python without
  compression.zstd pass through untouched.
Known difference for an asking client: an eligible response is held until its body is complete, so a handler that
fails after its start message now yields a clean 500 where it used to yield a truncated 200.

Desktop (apps/desktop/electron/delta-reads.ts): fetchJson asks on every GET to an SSH-tunnelled backend and keeps
the newest and the previous body per remote scope and path (48 MB / 96 reads, memory only). Per scope, so the
refetch after a tunnel rebuild is a delta when the backend process still remembers the base; the previous body, so
two reads in flight on the same base both decode. A delta is decoded against the base it names and its hash
verified; base not held, bad frame, wrong hash, missing hash or non-200 drops the read's copies and fails the
attempt with HERMES_DELTA_RETRY, which api-transport retries for a GET, now without a base. A torn-down tunnel is
unbound, so a reused local port is not asked. A transcript page read keeps its own slim+gzip ask. An older backend
ignores the headers.

Tests: tests/hermes_cli/test_web_delta_response.py (whole body + hash; delta decodes to the exact current body and
chains; unchanged body; unknown and evicted base; each ineligible response and HEAD untouched; writes; chunked
body with a Content-Length; other headers incl. duplicate Set-Cookie survive; per-credential isolation; encoding
failure; a no-smaller delta; one-field change at 0.5 and 3.5 MB; size bounds; no zstd; store bound; the middleware
sits inside the auth gates of the dashboard app) and apps/desktop/electron/delta-reads.test.ts (ask rules; chain of
deltas; a frame made by Python; tunnel rebuild and unbind; concurrent reads on one base; withRetry re-asking
without a base; every failure drops the copy; forged hash; what is never kept; pass-through; store bounds; and
fetchJson itself (json-fetch.ts) driven against a live server speaking the protocol: not asked until bound, whole
body once, then deltas, and a damaged frame retried for the whole body inside the same call). Checked by hand over real HTTP with
Desktop's Electron 44 as the client and a session-token credential: a 3.4 MB answer was 368 bytes on the wire,
twice in a chain.

